 | `present_snap` | `on` / `off` (the default): present snapshots by default (`nvn_mtl.m` `mtl_snap_on`, a ring of 2 host textures): each present is copied and the game's swapchain texture is free at once, so the GPU does not idle between frames ([Present snapshots](../../docs/reference/frame-rate.md#present-snapshots-the-games-swapchain-texture-free-at-once)). A setting the game was tested with: the iOS launcher's **Frame buffering** defaults to it, and `DM_PRESENT_SNAP` / `Documents/env.txt` override it. Breath of the Wild sets it on |
 | `pso_mtl4` | `on` / `off` (the default): on iOS, render pipelines through MTL4Compiler by default, with the pipeline archive, the prepare step, the hybrid pipelines and the prebuild (on by default on the Mac; `DM_PSO_MTL4=1` / `0` overrides). A setting the game was tested with on an iPhone. It also gives the app the [pipeline archive made offline](../../docs/development.md#the-pipeline-archive-made-offline) (`build_ipa.sh` builds it for `g18p` unless `DM_PSO_GPUS` says otherwise; without the key, none) and the pulling libraries (`_bp`). Breath of the Wild sets it on |
 | `enc_thread` | `on` / `off` (the default): on the Mac, Metal encoding on its own thread by default, as on iOS for every game (`nvn_rec.h` `rec_on`; `DM_NVN_ENC_THREAD=1` / `0` and the launcher's Encode thread override it). For a game that waits on its fence every frame, whose submit thread would otherwise also encode before the wait: Need for Speed: Hot Pursuit Remastered's 60 fps frame took 18.1 ms with encoding on the submit thread and 16.7 on its own |
-| `fps_target` | Frame rate cap of the rewriter's frame step, for builds that have one (`FRAME_STEP` in the profile's `patches.py`: Tears of the Kingdom 1.4.3, Breath of the Wild 1.9.0, Hyrule Warriors: Age of Calamity 1.3.0, Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door 1.0.1, ASTRAL CHAIN 1.0.1), or the engine's own frame limit the host writes into game memory (`hle_fpslock.c`: Monster Hunter Rise 16.0.2, which also needs `present_interval display`; any Unreal Engine 4.2x / 5 build, its `t.MaxFPS` (or `ue_fps_cvar`) and `rhi.SyncInterval` or `nvn.SyncInterval`, found by name by the rewriter, so the game asks for present interval 1 itself; Luigi's Mansion 3 1.4.0, its frame limiter's milliseconds per frame, with `present_interval display`; Pokémon Brilliant Diamond 1.3.0, Unity's integer `Application.targetFrameRate`, also with `present_interval display`; EA SPORTS FC 27, Frostbite's simulation rate, found by name on any build, and on 1.8d.310c its frame regulator, also with `present_interval display`). `0` (the default): the game's own step. `30` / `60`: the host decides each frame's step in whole vsyncs and keeps the frame on screen that long (`hle_nvn.c` `fstep_tick`, `nvn_fstep.h`): 1 at a 60 cap, 2 when 60 does not hold or at a 30 cap (and while a prerendered movie plays), plus one vsync while the game is behind real time after a late frame. Presents follow that hold instead of the interval the game sets. `DM_FPS_TARGET` overrides it, also from `Documents/env.txt`; the log line `frame step: game time … of real time` checks the game's speed |
+| `fps_target` | Frame rate cap of the rewriter's frame step, for builds that have one (`FRAME_STEP` in the profile's `patches.py`: Tears of the Kingdom 1.4.3, Breath of the Wild 1.9.0, Hyrule Warriors: Age of Calamity 1.3.0, Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door 1.0.1, ASTRAL CHAIN 1.0.1), or the engine's own frame limit the host writes into game memory (`hle_fpslock.c`: Monster Hunter Rise 16.0.2, which also needs `present_interval display`; any Unreal Engine 4.2x / 5 build, its `t.MaxFPS` (or `ue_fps_cvar`) and `rhi.SyncInterval` or `nvn.SyncInterval`, found by name by the rewriter, so the game asks for present interval 1 itself; Luigi's Mansion 3 1.4.0, its frame limiter's milliseconds per frame, with `present_interval display`; Pokémon Brilliant Diamond 1.3.0, Unity's integer `Application.targetFrameRate`, also with `present_interval display`; EA SPORTS FC 27, Frostbite's simulation rate, found by name on any build, and on 1.8d.310c its frame regulator, also with `present_interval display`). `0` (the default): the game's own step. `30` / `60`: the host decides each frame's step in whole vsyncs and keeps the frame on screen that long (`hle_nvn.c` `fstep_tick`, `nvn_fstep.h`): 1 at a 60 cap, 2 when 60 does not hold or at a 30 cap (and while a prerendered movie plays), plus one vsync while the game is behind real time after a late frame. Presents follow that hold instead of the interval the game sets. `DM_FPS_TARGET` overrides it, also from `Documents/env.txt`; the log line `frame step: game time … of real time` checks the game's speed. A derived profile (`derive_profile`) whose `main` has no `FRAME_STEP` stubs, while the profile has them for other builds, gets `fps_target 0`: without the stubs the host would present every vsync and the game would run at double speed |
 | `fps_hold` | `on` (the default): with `fps_target`, three late frames in a row raise the hold above the cap's vsyncs (30 or 20 fps), and steady frames that fit try the lower hold again. `off`: frames are never held longer than the cap's vsyncs; each is shown as soon as it is ready. For a game whose updates do not wait for the presents and step by the cap whatever the hold (Age of Calamity): there the hold only paces the screen. `DM_FPS_HOLD=on` / `off` overrides it, also from `Documents/env.txt` Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door sets it off too: its frame waits for three render worker threads, and holding frames left them idle, so a frame cost more the longer the hold (the hold stuck at 3 vsyncs, 20 fps, on a loaded Mac; 32–58 fps without holds) |
 | `ue_fps_cvar` | Unreal Engine games: the console variable the host writes `fps_target` into (`hle_fpslock.c`). `t.MaxFPS` (the default) is the engine's frame limit; a game whose settings apply a variable of its own to `t.MaxFPS` names that one, or the next settings change sets the game's 30 again (SHIN MEGAMI TENSEI V: Vengeance: `Project.FrameRateLimit`). The rewriter finds it by its registered name, so a name set here must be in `dm/tools/ue_cvar.py`'s `NAMES` (a red line otherwise): [Unreal's frame limit and present interval, found by name](../../docs/reference/frame-rate.md#unreals-frame-limit-and-present-interval-found-by-name) |
 | `ue_texture_pool` | Unreal Engine games: megabytes of the texture streaming pool (`r.Streaming.PoolSize`) the host raises the game's own to, on the Mac (`hle_uecvar.c`; the rewriter finds the variable by its name, `dm/tools/ue_cvar.py`): past its pool the streamer drops the top mips of what is in view, and the Switch's pool is small (DRAGON QUEST VII Reimagined: 200 MB, 1000 on Windows). `0` (the default): the game's own pool. A larger value the game sets, or −1, stays. `DM_UE_TEXTURE_POOL` overrides it; on iOS only that switch turns it on (`Documents/env.txt`). An improvement over the Switch, not a fix: [Unreal's texture streaming pool](../../docs/reference/rendering.md#unreals-texture-streaming-pool) |
diff --git a/dm/tools/tests/test_nsp_incremental.py b/dm/tools/tests/test_nsp_incremental.py
index 66db552..4387057 100644
--- a/dm/tools/tests/test_nsp_incremental.py
